{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a razor-sharp dissection of ambition, betrayal, and the birth of an era—all wrapped in the sleek, biting dialogue of aaron sorkin and the icy visuals of director david fincher. the film chronicles the turbulent founding of facebook through the eyes of its controversial creator, mark zuckerberg (played with a chilling mix of detachment and intensity by jesse eisenberg), as he transforms a dorm-room project into a global phenomenon while alienating nearly everyone in his path.\n\nthe script crackles with wit, packing every scene with rapid-fire exchanges that feel both hyper-real and cinematically heightened. sorkin’s dialogue is a masterclass in exposition done right—complex legal and technical jargon flows naturally, making the film accessible without dumbing anything down. lines like *\"you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd. but it’ll be because you’re an asshole\"* land with brutal precision, revealing character in the most economical way possible.\n\neisenberg’s performance is the film’s anchor, portraying zuckerberg as a socially stunted genius whose brilliance is matched only by his inability to connect emotionally. his physicality—jerky, almost robotic in its precision—contrasts with the raw vulnerability he occasionally lets slip, particularly in scenes with his best friend (and eventual legal foe) eduardo savarin (andrew garfield), whose betrayal by zuckerberg is one of the film’s most heartbreaking moments. armie hammer’s dual performance as the winklevoss twins is a standout, delivering a perfect blend of smug entitlement and physical comedy, while brenda song and malese jow as zuckerberg’s early romantic interest and later corporate adversary add layers of complexity to the film’s exploration of power and manipulation.\n\nfincher’s direction is a marvel of modern filmmaking, blending cold, clinical aesthetics with bursts of emotional intensity. the use of natural lighting and muted color palettes gives the film a timeless quality, while the editing (by kirk baxter and angus wall) is a marvel of pacing, maintaining a relentless momentum that mirrors the breakneck speed of the digital revolution. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is a haunting electronic soundscape that underscores the film’s themes of isolation and control.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a biopic—it’s a cautionary tale about the cost of success, the myth of the \"lone genius,\" and the fragility of human relationships in the face of ambition. it’s also a scathing indictment of the tech industry’s early days, where idealism quickly curdled into cutthroat capitalism. the film’s most damning critique isn’t about facebook itself, but about the people who built it: flawed, insecure, and often cruel individuals who convinced themselves they were changing the world while barely understanding the consequences.\n\nthe only minor quibble is that the film occasionally glosses over some of the more nuanced legal battles (particularly regarding the winklevoss twins' lawsuit) in favor of dramatic license, but this is a minor complaint in an otherwise flawless film. the social network is a modern classic—a film that feels just as relevant today as it did in 2010, if not more so, given the dominance of social media in our daily lives.\n\nin short: a masterpiece of modern cinema, combining brilliant writing, powerhouse performances, and a visual style that’s as precise as it is evocative. it’s a film that demands to be watched, dissected, and rewatched, not just for its entertainment value but for its razor-sharp insights into the digital age. 10/10.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0027128581423312426}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "log